 \section{Gaze Quality and Feedback Coupling}
 Gaze serves both as an input to assistance and as a recorded behavioral signal. Tracking gaps can change the participant's experience and reduce measurement coverage, affecting both the outcome being measured and the evidence available to interpret it. Coverage and technical events will therefore be considered alongside performance without automatically being treated as pre-existing participant characteristics.
 
-A highlighted vicinity candidate can help indicate what the system currently attends to, but it can also suggest a reference object that differs from the mathematical gaze ray. For example, an instruction to look lower can be consistent with a gaze ray above the target yet confusing if the target appears higher than a highlighted neighbor; Appendix~\ref{app:guidance-support} describes this reference-frame failure. A cue should be evaluated in its visible interaction context, not solely against the sign of a geometric delta. Development-only rings and diagnostic markers must not silently enter the participant condition.
+A highlighted vicinity candidate can help indicate what the system currently attends to, but it can also suggest a reference object that differs from the mathematical gaze ray. For example, an instruction to look lower can be consistent with a gaze ray above the target yet confusing if the target appears higher than a highlighted neighbor. A cue should be evaluated in its visible interaction context, not solely against the sign of a geometric delta. Development-only rings and diagnostic markers must not silently enter the participant condition.
 
 The proposed validity-aware stream and independent calibration checks address these risks, but headset-specific spatial performance and the rate of distinct gaze observations remain empirical questions. Validation results for the Vive Pro Eye cannot substitute for Focus Vision measurements, and an interaction threshold chosen for usability does not establish a validated measure of cognition.
 
